L2(6,'oceans-and-continents','Oceans and Continents',
 ('The blue planet','Early astronauts looked back at the Earth and called it the blue planet. Almost three-fourths of its surface is covered with water, and the rest is land. In this chapter we meet the five oceans, the seven continents and the islands in between. We also see how oceans give us rain and oxygen, and how they can bring storms and tsunamis.'),
 [('Water and land on the Earth',['Rotate a globe slowly, or look at a picture of the Earth taken from the Moon. The most widespread colour is blue, and blue stands for water. Almost three-fourths of the surface is water, which is why the Earth is called the blue planet.','The largest water bodies are called oceans. Their smaller extensions have names such as sea, bay and gulf.','The brown colour in the picture is land, a little over one-fourth of the globe. A large body of land is a landmass, and a large continuous expanse of land is a continent.','Oceans and continents are not spread equally between the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. Both of them shape the climate and affect plants, animals and people.','The Indian Navy badge carries the motto Sam noh Varunah, meaning, Be auspicious to us, O Varuna. Varuna is a Vedic deity linked with the oceans, the sky and water.','Seawater is salty and unfit for most land animals, including humans. Fresh water is a very small part of all the water on the planet. It is found in glaciers, rivers, lakes, the atmosphere and underground, where it is called groundwater. That is why people speak of a water crisis even though the planet has so much water.']),
  ('The five oceans',['The world map shows five oceans: the Pacific, the Atlantic, the Indian, the Arctic and the Southern or Antarctic Ocean.','The lines that divide them are only conventions. In nature the oceans are joined, and seawater keeps flowing from one to another carrying marine life.','Marine flora includes tiny plants called algae and many kinds of seaweed. Marine fauna includes thousands of kinds of fish, dolphins, whales and many deep-sea creatures.','By size, the Pacific is the largest, then the Atlantic, then the Indian Ocean third and the Southern Ocean fourth. The Arctic is the smallest.','The Indian Ocean has Asia to the north, Africa to the west and Australia to the east, with the Southern Ocean to the south. On either side of India lie two parts of it: the Arabian Sea in the west and the Bay of Bengal in the east.']),
  ('Oceans and disasters',['White shapes in the picture of the Earth are clouds. They bring rain to the continents. The monsoon rains that India expects each summer begin over the ocean, and without them our farming and all life would suffer.','Oceans also give rise to violent storms with extreme rain or strong winds, such as cyclones, which can badly damage coasts.','A tsunami is a huge, powerful wave, usually caused by a strong earthquake or a volcanic eruption on the ocean floor. It can travel thousands of kilometres and flood coastal areas.','On 26 December 2004 an earthquake near Indonesia caused a tsunami that struck India and 13 other countries around the Indian Ocean. More than two lakh people died. The Andaman and Nicobar Islands and the coasts of Tamil Nadu and Kerala were badly hit.','Tsunamis are rare, but they can often be detected before they reach a coast. Many countries, including India, take part in the Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning System, and India has a National Disaster Management Authority to handle disasters.']),
  ('The continents',['How many continents are there? The answer depends on how we count. We can count anywhere from four to seven.','North America and South America are usually two continents, but they can be seen as one landmass. Europe and Asia are usually two, although the map shows one landmass, which geologists often call Eurasia. Africa and Eurasia are usually two but are sometimes counted as one.','Counting four gives Africa-Eurasia, America, Antarctica and Australia. Five gives Africa, America, Antarctica, Australia and Eurasia. Six splits America into North and South America. Seven, the most widely used, gives Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America and South America.','The five Olympic rings stand for the five inhabited continents: Africa, America, Asia, Australia and Europe.']),
  ('Islands',['The continents do not include all the land. Smaller pieces of land surrounded by water on all sides are called islands. Continents are also surrounded by water, but they are too large to be called islands.','There are lakhs of islands of very different sizes. Greenland is the largest island in the world.','India has more than 1300 small islands. There are two major groups: the Andaman and Nicobar Islands in the Bay of Bengal and the Lakshadweep Islands in the Arabian Sea.','Since 1981 the Indian Antarctica Programme has explored Antarctica, a continent with a very cold climate. India set up its first scientific base there in 1983, called Dakshin Gangotri, and later two more bases. About 40 teams of Indian scientists have studied climate and environment there.']),
  ('Oceans and life, and how we can protect them',['Oceans send rain to the continents as part of the Earth water cycle. Without oceans there would be no rainfall and the Earth would be a desert.','More than half of the world oxygen is produced by the plants of the oceans, which is why oceans are called the lungs of the planet. They help regulate the climate and sustain life.','From early times people used oceans and seas to migrate, to trade, to carry out military campaigns and for food from fishing. Coastal peoples everywhere have tales of sea gods, sea monsters and treasures from the sea.','The United Nations has marked 8 June as World Oceans Day.','Human activity pollutes the oceans. Several million tonnes of plastic waste reach them every year and choke marine life. Overfishing, which means catching too much fish, also reduces marine life.','Protecting the oceans is our collective responsibility for the future of the planet and of humanity.'])],
 [('The oceans in size order: from the biggest to the smallest',[('Start with the largest, which is the Pacific.','The Pacific is the largest ocean in the world.'),('Next comes the Atlantic.','The Atlantic is second in size.'),('Then the Indian Ocean third and the Southern Ocean fourth.','These come after the Atlantic.'),('The smallest is the Arctic Ocean.','The Arctic is the fifth and smallest.')])],
 ('Explain how oceans help us and how they can also harm us.',['Oceans cover almost three-fourths of the Earth surface and send rain through the water cycle','Their plants give more than half of the world oxygen','Oceans give food, trade routes and cultures but can bring cyclones and tsunamis','People pollute oceans with plastic and overfish, so we must protect them'],['Thinking oceans are separate and do not mix','Thinking seawater can be drunk like fresh water','Thinking there are exactly seven continents in every count']),
 ['Almost three-fourths of the Earth is water and a little over one-fourth is land.','The five oceans are the Pacific, Atlantic, Indian, Southern and Arctic, and they are all joined.','Continents can be counted as four, five, six or seven; seven is most used.','Islands are small landmasses surrounded by water. India has more than 1300, in the Andaman and Nicobar and the Lakshadweep groups.','Oceans give rain, oxygen, food, trade routes and culture, but also cyclones and tsunamis, and pollution and overfishing threaten them.'],
 [('m','What part of the Earth surface is covered by water?','Almost three-fourths',[('About one-fourth','One-fourth is roughly the land, not the water.'),('About half','Water covers far more than half the surface.')],'Almost three-fourths of the surface is water, which is why the Earth looks blue from space.'),
  ('m','Which is the largest ocean?','The Pacific Ocean',[('The Atlantic Ocean','The Atlantic is the second largest.'),('The Indian Ocean','The Indian Ocean is third in size.')],'The Pacific is the largest, then the Atlantic, the Indian, the Southern and the Arctic.'),
  ('m','The two parts of the Indian Ocean on either side of India are','the Arabian Sea and the Bay of Bengal',[('the Pacific and the Atlantic','These are separate oceans far from India.'),('the Arctic Sea and the Southern Sea','These lie near the poles, not beside India.')],'The Arabian Sea is to the west and the Bay of Bengal to the east of India.'),
  ('t','Freshwater makes up most of the water on the planet.',False,'Most water is salty ocean water, and fresh water is a very small share.'),
  ('m','A tsunami is usually caused by','an earthquake or volcanic eruption on the ocean floor',[('the Moon turning dark','The Moon does not cause tsunamis.'),('monsoon winds blowing over land','Monsoon winds bring rain, not tsunami waves.')],'A strong undersea earthquake or eruption pushes up a huge wave.'),
  ('m','In the most widely used count, how many continents are there?','Seven',[('Four','Four is possible only if America and Africa-Eurasia are joined.'),('Five','Five joins North and South America and also Europe and Asia.')],'The seven-continent count is the one most adopted.'),
  ('m','Which is the largest island in the world?','Greenland',[('Lakshadweep','Lakshadweep is a small island group in the Arabian Sea.'),('Andaman','The Andaman Islands are a group in the Bay of Bengal, much smaller than Greenland.')],'Greenland is the largest island in the world.'),
  ('m','Why are the oceans called the lungs of the planet?','Their plants produce more than half of the world oxygen',[('They pump air through the sky','Oceans do not pump air.'),('They are shaped like lungs','The name comes from oxygen production, not shape.')],'Marine plants such as algae produce more than half the oxygen on Earth.'),
  ('m','India first scientific base in Antarctica, set up in 1983, is called','Dakshin Gangotri',[('Himadri','Himadri is an Indian station in the Arctic, not the first one in Antarctica.'),('Lakshadweep','Lakshadweep is an Indian island group.')],'Dakshin Gangotri was the first Indian base in Antarctica.'),
  ('t','On 26 December 2004 a tsunami started by an earthquake in Indonesia hit India.',True,'It struck India and 13 other countries, including the Andaman and Nicobar Islands and the coasts of Tamil Nadu and Kerala.'),
  ('m','Why is the line between two oceans on a map only a convention?','Seawater flows across it and the oceans are joined',[('The oceans are exactly the same','The oceans differ in size and names, but they are connected.'),('There are no oceans','Oceans are real, but the dividing lines are drawn by people.')],'The oceans form one connected body of water and marine life moves between them.'),
  ('m','Which two are major human-made threats to the oceans?','Plastic pollution and overfishing',[('Rainfall and clouds','Rain and clouds are natural parts of the water cycle.'),('Tides and waves','Tides and waves are natural movements of water.')],'Plastic waste chokes marine life and overfishing reduces fish stocks.')],
 keep=['ocean','continent','island','tsunami'],svgs=[('c6-continent-counts','A table with four ways of counting continents: four, five, six and seven','Continents can be counted in four ways')])
